    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/NLnM7/Director-Future-Students,2026-25358.html": {
      "title": "Director Future Students",
      "description": "Director Future Students -\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Director Future Students Lead the end-to-end student recruitment, admissions, marketing and scholarship functions to grow enrolments and drive student conversion. Mō te tūka | The role Based in our Future Students division, this pivotal leadership role is responsible for overseeing Lincoln University's student recruitment and admissions pipeline, from first engagement through to enrolment. Reporting to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Student Life, you will provide strategic leadership across Domestic Recruitment, International Recruitment, Admissions & Enrolments, Marketing and Scholarships, creating a seamless student journey and driving recruitment growth across domestic and international markets. Some tasks of the role include: Leading the end-to-end student recruitment, admissions and conversion strategy. Driving domestic and international enrolment growth and EFTS performance. Overseeing marketing, recruitment, admissions and scholarship activities within a single integrated pipeline. Using data, market intelligence and forecasting to improve recruitment outcomes and inform strategic planning. Building and maintaining strong relationships with faculties, schools, agents, pathway providers and external partners. Leading and developing a high-performing team focused on student recruitment, marketing, admissions and conversion success. Working collaboratively across the University, you will use data, market insights and innovative recruitment strategies to achieve enrolment targets, strengthen the student experience and contribute directly to Lincoln University's long-term sustainability and success. Mahi & Wāhi | Work pattern & location This permanent role is based at our Lincoln Campus and works 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE). Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role You will bring: Significant senior leadership experience in student recruitment, admissions, marketing or a related field. A proven track record delivering recruitment, conversion and growth outcomes in a complex organisation. Experience leading integrated recruitment and admissions functions with accountability across the full student pipeline. Strong analytical capability and experience using CRM and student management systems to drive data-informed decisions. Excellent stakeholder engagement, relationship management and communication skills, with the ability to influence at senior levels. Strong commercial and financial acumen, including budget management, strategic planning and performance leadership. Alignment with Manaakitaka and Lincoln University values. Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Generous leave entitlements – six weeks of paid leave per year (based on 1.0 FTE) Annual salary reviews to recognise your contribution Contributions toward eye tests and prescription eyewear Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to careers@lincoln.ac.nz We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels valued and supported, and we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We are only considering applicants who have current unrestricted legal working rights in New Zealand. We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this role. View the position description at: https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz Job Details Reference # 2026-25358 Posted on 02 Sep 2026 Closes on 16 Sep 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise General Mgmt/Executive Work type(s) Permanent full-time More details (document) PD Director Future Students September 2026.pdf Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 6 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
    },
    "https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz/jobdetails/ajid/dRnM7/Inclusive-Education-Advisor,2026-25359.html": {
      "title": "Inclusive Education Advisor",
      "description": "Inclusive Education Advisor -\n        Lincoln University Sign in Search jobs Search Show all jobs Keyword or Job Code Locations Any Work type Any Expertise Any show advanced Search careers@lincoln.ac.nz Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Inclusive Education Advisor Support students with disabilities, chronic illness or injury by coordinating tailored support, advocacy and inclusive learning solutions. Mō te tūka | The role Based in our Inclusive Education team, this role is all about helping students with a disability, chronic illness or injury achieve success in their studies. You'll work directly with students to assess support needs, develop individual support plans, coordinate accommodations, and remove barriers to learning. Working closely with academic and professional staff, you'll play a key role in creating an accessible and inclusive learning environment while contributing to the ongoing development of Inclusive Education services. Some tasks of the role include: Coordinating tailored support and accommodations for students Developing and implementing Individual Support Plans Providing guidance, advocacy and problem-solving support Collaborating with academic and professional staff to support student success Contributing to continuous improvement and accessibility initiatives Maintaining accurate records, reporting and student support systems Mahi & Wāhi / Utu | Work pattern, location & salary This permanent role is based at our Lincoln Campus and works 37.5 hours per week (1.0 FTE). The salary for this position is $65,000 - $70,000 per annum, depending on experience. Kā tino Pūkeka/Whēako | Essential skills/experience for the role You will bring: A relevant tertiary qualification in education, disability support, inclusion, mental health or a related field, or equivalent experience Experience supporting people with disabilities, chronic illness or injury, ideally within an education environment Knowledge of disability support frameworks, inclusive practices and relevant legislation Experience assessing needs and developing personalised support plans Strong communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to work effectively with diverse stakeholders Excellent problem-solving skills, sound judgement and confidence using technology and student management systems Kā hua ka riro ki a koe | What's in it for you? Join a supportive, inclusive community that values diversity, wellbeing, and professional growth. Benefits include: Flexible, family‑friendly work + free on‑site parking Professional development & mentorship to grow your career Generous leave entitlements – six weeks of paid leave per year (based on 1.0 FTE) Annual salary reviews to recognise your contribution Contributions toward eye tests and prescription eyewear Free, confidential wellbeing support through Clearhead (EAP) Southern Cross employee benefits Access to on‑campus gym with discounted membership On‑site early childhood centre with staff discounts A vibrant campus lifestyle with cafés, green spaces, and a sense of community Mō mātou | About us Te Whare Wānaka o Aoraki | Lincoln University is a specialist institution dedicated to impactful research and higher education that supports land-based industries in New Zealand and globally. Our campus sits at the heart of the Lincoln research precinct, complemented by university-owned farms and research stations that blend education, research, and hands-on learning. At Lincoln, students are at the heart of everything we do, empower them to grow their potential and contribute to a more sustainably productive world through world-class teaching and research. As a progressive university with an ambitious vision, we value a professional workforce that reflects the diversity of our community. Our staff engage with students, parents, government, iwi, and external stakeholders, upholding Lincoln University’s reputation through their work. Our Te Tiriti o Waitangi commitments begin with mana whenua. We honour our special relationship with Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki and Te Taumutu Rūnanga, embedded in our He Tūtohinga Whakamātau | Charter of Understanding. Tono ināianei | Apply now Ready to apply? Submit your CV and a cover letter that highlights your fit - tell us why you want this role and what motivates you to join Lincoln University. Applications received via email will not be considered, however specific enquiry relating to the role can be made to rose.edgar@lincoln.ac.nz We are committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive community where everyone feels valued and supported, and we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. We are only considering applicants who have current unrestricted legal working rights in New Zealand. We are unable to provide visa sponsorship for this role. View the position description at: https://careers.lincoln.ac.nz Job Details Reference # 2026-25359 Posted on 01 Sep 2026 Closes on 23 Sep 2026 18:00 NZST Location(s) Lincoln University Campus Expertise Administration, Customer Service Work type(s) Permanent full-time More details (document) Print Email this job to a friend Apply Apply with Seek Back © Lincoln University Agency sign in Powered by SnapHire | Privacy policy 6 This site is powered by Snaphire (www.snaphire.com) This site uses Javascript and is required to search and apply for jobs. Your browser either doesn't support Javascript or it is disabled. If you are on a Corporate Network please contact your Administrator for further assistance. Loading . . ."
